Heterogeneous agents and the A2A protocol
Real enterprises don't build every agent the same way. One team ships an ADK agent, another already has a LangGraph service, a third wants a managed runtime, and a fourth needs Kubernetes. A useful platform lets those agents cooperate without forcing everyone onto one framework or one runtime. That is what this lab demonstrates, and the Agent2Agent (A2A) protocol is what makes it possible.

A2A gives every agent a common contract, regardless of how it is built:

Agent card: a small JSON document at a well-known path (/.well-known/agent-card.json or /a2a/app/.well-known/agent-card.json) that describes the agent's skills and its endpoint. A caller reads the card, then sends messages to the endpoint. The caller never needs to know the callee's framework.
Transport: the actual calls use JSON-RPC over HTTP. Because the contract is HTTP + JSON, an ADK agent, a LangGraph server, and a managed engine all look the same to a caller.
A2A has variations you will meet in this lab, and knowing them explains several choices you make:

The ADK A2A extension. When an ADK agent calls another ADK agent, it can opt into an ADK-specific extension (use_legacy=False) that carries richer event data. When it calls a non-ADK server, it uses plain, standard A2A. You will see both in the root agent.
Protocol versions. The a2a-sdk library changed its card shape between the 0.3 and 1.x releases. ADK currently pins the older 0.3 client, so a server built on a2a-sdk 1.x must advertise a 0.3-compatible card or the ADK client cannot parse it. The Stack Exchange agent does exactly this in Task 3.
These variants come together in one comparison. You apply this when you wire the root to the specialists in Task 5:

Dimension	What it means	In this lab
Agent card	JSON at a well-known path describing skills + endpoint	Every specialist publishes one
Transport	JSON-RPC over HTTP for the calls	All use JSON-RPC
Standard vs ADK extension	ADK agents can opt into the ADK A2A extension for richer event data	github, salesforce, bq-agent use it; stackexchange does not
Version (1.x vs 0.3)	a2a-sdk major versions differ in card shape	stackexchange serves 1.x with a 0.3-compatible card
Serving style	self-served (a2a-sdk) vs scaffolded (agents-cli/ADK)	stackexchange self-serves; github, salesforce, bq-agent are scaffolded
Keep one idea in mind throughout: a specialist is just an agent card at a URL. That is why you can mix frameworks and runtimes freely, and why extending the system later (Task 7) is so easy.

Authentication across runtimes
Because the specialists live on different runtimes, the root agent authenticates to each one differently. This is not incidental complexity; it reflects how each runtime secures traffic, and it drives real steps in this lab.

An A2A call, per-host authentication, and authorization
Every A2A call resolves the target's agent card, then sends a message; the credential the caller must attach depends on the target's runtime, and the target still checks whether the caller is authorized before it returns 200 OK.

Agent Runtime (GitHub, root): endpoints are always authenticated. A caller presents a Google OAuth access token. Inside a deployed agent, the Agent Platform SDK's Application Default Credentials (ADC) provide that token as the agent's runtime service account automatically. Locally, ADC resolves to your user credentials. The same code works in both places.
Cloud Run (Stack Exchange, Salesforce): services can be public or private. A public service needs no token. A private service needs a Google identity token whose audience is the service URL, and the caller needs roles/run.invoker.
GKE (Bug DB): authentication depends on how you expose the service. A public load balancer needs no token.
There is one more layer: even with a valid token, the caller's identity must be authorized to invoke the target. A missing token yields 401 Unauthorized; a valid token without permission yields 403 Permission Denied. In Task 6 you grant the root's service account permission to invoke the GitHub agent for exactly this reason.

Design choice: Agent Runtime also offers an agent identity mode (a dedicated SPIFFE identity brokered by Agent Gateway). This lab uses the simpler, well-understood model instead: the runtime service account plus an ADC access token and an explicit IAM grant. It works identically on your machine and when deployed, which keeps the focus on A2A rather than identity infrastructure.
Beyond authenticating each call, a production platform also governs which agents may talk to which. That is what the agent-identity mode enables: Agent Gateway authenticates every agent by its agent identity (a SPIFFE X.509 identity, with mutual TLS and DPoP for end-to-end proof) and enforces IAM allow/deny policies before traffic reaches the target. Source agents are principals, target services are Agent Registry resources, and an allow policy grants roles/iap.egressor on the target (optionally narrowed with CEL conditions).

Agent Gateway policy enforcement Agent Gateway authenticates each agent's identity and enforces IAM allow/deny before traffic reaches the target. This lab uses the lighter runtime-service-account model; Agent Gateway is the heavier production path for governing a fleet.

