Task 2. Deploy the GitHub agent with ADK and agents-cli
In this task, you deploy your first specialist. The GitHub agent is a standard ADK agent whose only tool is a hosted MCP (Model Context Protocol) server for GitHub. You deploy it to Agent Runtime and see how little stands between ADK code and a running, A2A-enabled agent.

Why Agent Runtime for this agent? Agent Runtime is Google's managed runtime for agents. You hand it your code; it builds the container, runs it, serves the A2A card and JSON-RPC endpoint, and manages sessions. It is the fastest path from ADK code to a running agent, which is why the lab starts here.

Why MCP instead of hand-written tools? MCP lets an agent use tools hosted by a separate server. The GitHub tools live on a remote MCP endpoint (https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/), so there is no tool code to write, package, or maintain in this agent. This also means the agent behaves the same locally and when deployed, because the tools are always remote.

Create a GitHub personal access token
The GitHub agent's only tool is a hosted GitHub MCP server, which authenticates with a personal access token (PAT). The agent uses only read tools, so a read-only token is enough. In this subtask, you create a classic PAT.

Go to GitHub personal access tokens (classic).

Select Generate new token, then Generate new token (classic).

For Note, type code-assist-lab, and choose an Expiration.

Under Select scopes, select repo and read:org.

Select Generate token, then copy the ghp_... value. This is your GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN.

Important: Copy the token now. GitHub shows it only once. Save this token in a secure location, as it will be needed in the next labs.
Configure the agent
In the Cloud Shell Terminal, create the agent's .env file.

cd ~/agents/github-agent
cat << EOF > .env
GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=PROJECT_ID
GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION=global
GOOGLE_GENAI_USE_VERTEXAI=True
MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash
GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN=PASTE_YOUR_TOKEN
GITHUB_MCP_URL=https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/
EOF
Copied!
Open ~/agents/github-agent/.env in the editor and replace PASTE_YOUR_TOKEN with the token you created. Leave the other values as they are.

Tip: .env is a hidden file. In the Cloud Shell Editor, enable View > Toggle Hidden Files to see it.
Configure the agent to use the token and connect to the GitHub MCP Server
In the editor, open ~/agents/github-agent/app/agent.py.

Complete the TODO to assign a value to github_mcp_toolset. This should be set to an McpToolset which uses StreamableHTTPConnectionParams to connect to the GITHUB_MCP_URL. Headers should be configured to use your PAT stored as GITHUB_TOKEN as an authorization header. You should also use a tool_filter to restrict the tools to those included in the GITHUB_TOOL_FILTER list.


Using a tool_filter is a good habit. It narrows the agent's capabilities to the minimum it needs, which improves safety, latency, and the model's tool-selection accuracy.

Deploy the agent
Set the project and region variables for this task (re-run these if you opened a new Cloud Shell tab).

export PROJECT_ID=PROJECT_ID
export REGION=us-central1
gcloud config set project ${PROJECT_ID}
Copied!
Deploy the agent to Agent Runtime. The command reads the variables from .env and injects them, excluding GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT because --project sets it.

agents-cli deploy --project ${PROJECT_ID} --no-confirm-project --no-wait \
  --update-env-vars "$(grep -v '^#' .env | grep -v '^$' | grep -v '^GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT' | paste -sd,)"
Copied!
Deploy failing with an auth error? If you see service account info is missing 'email' field, your Application Default Credentials lapsed. Run gcloud auth application-default login, then re-run the deploy. If you see a gcloud reauthentication or "no active account" error instead, run gcloud auth login and re-run the deploy. If the failure persists, click Restart in the Cloud Shell toolbar and re-run this task's export block first.
Poll until the deployment reports success (this takes 5-10 minutes).

agents-cli deploy --status
Copied!
Note: A2A is enabled automatically (is_a2a: true in agents-cli-manifest.yaml), so the deployed agent serves its agent card and JSON-RPC without any extra flag. A transient code 13 INTERNAL on the first attempt usually succeeds on retry.
Record the printed Agent Card URL in an environment variable, and persist it to ~/.bashrc so it survives a new Cloud Shell tab. You use it later as GITHUB_AGENT_URL.

export GITHUB_AGENT_URL="PASTE_THE_AGENT_CARD_URL_HERE"
echo "export GITHUB_AGENT_URL='${GITHUB_AGENT_URL}'" >> ~/.bashrc
Copied!
Test the agent
Call the deployed agent's A2A card. Agent Runtime is always authenticated, so you pass a bearer token. The card is the A2A contract: it advertises the agent's skills and its JSON-RPC endpoint.

curl -sS -H "Authorization: Bearer $(gcloud auth print-access-token)" "${GITHUB_AGENT_URL}" | python3 -m json.tool
Copied!
Derive the agent's run endpoint from the card URL, and print it to confirm it resolved.

export GITHUB_ENGINE_URL=$(echo "${GITHUB_AGENT_URL}" | sed -E 's#/reasoningEngines/v1/#/v1/#; s#/api/.*##')
echo "${GITHUB_ENGINE_URL}"
Copied!
Send a message to the deployed agent, and confirm it returns a repository result. Because this agent runs on Agent Runtime, use --mode adk; agents-cli detects your credentials automatically.

agents-cli run --url "${GITHUB_ENGINE_URL}" --mode adk "Find the google/adk-python repository"
Copied!
Why not the playground? agents-cli playground and agents-cli run without --url run the agent locally from your source. Passing --url targets the deployed agent instead, which is what you verify here.
Click Check my progress to verify the objective.
Deploy the GitHub agent to Agent Runtime

Task 3. Add a LangGraph specialist over A2A
In this task, you deploy a specialist that is not an ADK agent. The Stack Exchange agent is a LangGraph graph wrapped in a small a2a-sdk server, deployed to Cloud Run. To the rest of the system it is just another A2A card, which is the whole point: the framework behind a card doesn't matter to the caller.

Why this matters. This is the payoff of A2A. Your organization can keep its existing LangGraph (or any other framework) services and still plug them into an ADK orchestrator, as long as each one publishes an A2A card. You do not rewrite working agents to join the system.

Why Cloud Run? This agent is a plain container that serves HTTP on a port. Cloud Run runs exactly that with managed HTTPS and scale-to-zero, so it is a natural fit for a self-hosted A2A server that isn't an ADK/agents-cli project.

Update the agent
In the Cloud Shell Editor, open ~/agents/stackexchange-agent/app/agent.py.
Notice that the search() function runs a Stack Exchange search with no LLM. The search is deterministic, so this graph will need no model. That's a reminder that an "agent" node can be plain code.
Complete the TODO in the file to add the search node to the graph and connect the edges from START -> search -> END.
Update the a2a-sdk server
In the Cloud Shell Editor, open ~/agents/stackexchange-agent/app/server.py.

Notice that supported_interfaces is currently an empty list.

Update the supported_interfaces list to include an AgentInterface for both a 1.0 and a 0.3 protocol version, each for the same url of rpc_url and protocol_binding of JSONRPC.

Why the dual-version card? This server is built on a2a-sdk 1.x, whose default card moves the endpoint into a new field that an older client can't read. ADK currently pins the a2a-sdk 0.3 client, so if this card advertised only the 1.x shape, the root agent could not parse it. Advertising a 0.3-compatible interface (and enabling the 0.3 compatibility layer on the server) lets the ADK client consume it. This is the most common real-world A2A interop issue: a version mismatch between the caller's client and the callee's server.
Build the image and deploy to Cloud Run
Set the project and region variables for this task (re-run these if you opened a new Cloud Shell tab).

export PROJECT_ID=PROJECT_ID
export REGION=us-central1
gcloud config set project ${PROJECT_ID}
Copied!
Build the container image with Cloud Build and push it to Artifact Registry.

cd ~/agents/stackexchange-agent
gcloud builds submit --tag ${REGION}-docker.pkg.dev/${PROJECT_ID}/agents/stackexchange-agent:latest
Copied!
Deploy the image to Cloud Run. Public access (--allow-unauthenticated) keeps this specialist reachable without a token, matching a public search service.

gcloud run deploy stackexchange-agent \
  --image ${REGION}-docker.pkg.dev/${PROJECT_ID}/agents/stackexchange-agent:latest \
  --region ${REGION} --allow-unauthenticated --port 8080
Copied!
Deploy failing with an auth error? If you see service account info is missing 'email' field, your Application Default Credentials lapsed. Run gcloud auth application-default login, then re-run the deploy. If you see a gcloud reauthentication or "no active account" error instead, run gcloud auth login and re-run the deploy. If the failure persists, click Restart in the Cloud Shell toolbar and re-run this task's export block first.
Read the service URL and set it as PUBLIC_URL so the agent card advertises a reachable endpoint.

export SE_URL=$(gcloud run services describe stackexchange-agent --region ${REGION} --format='value(status.url)')
gcloud run services update stackexchange-agent --region ${REGION} --set-env-vars PUBLIC_URL=${SE_URL}
echo ${SE_URL}
Copied!
Test the agent
Set the card URL, persist it to ~/.bashrc, and fetch the card. Because the service is public, no token is needed.

export STACKEXCHANGE_AGENT_URL="${SE_URL}/.well-known/agent-card.json"
echo "export STACKEXCHANGE_AGENT_URL='${STACKEXCHANGE_AGENT_URL}'" >> ~/.bashrc
curl -sS "${STACKEXCHANGE_AGENT_URL}" | python3 -m json.tool
Copied!
Confirm the card lists the Stack Exchange skill and both interface versions.

Click Check my progress to verify the objective.
Deploy the Stack Exchange agent to Cloud Run

Task 4. Generate the Bug DB agent with Antigravity and deploy to GKE
In this task, you go faster with vibecoding. Instead of writing the Bug DB agent by hand, you give a specification to the Antigravity coding agent and let it generate a working ADK project. The agent runs semantic vector search over a BigQuery table of past incidents, which is already populated for you. You deploy it to GKE.

Why generate it? The earlier agents showed how little code A2A and ADK require. A precise specification is now enough for a coding agent to produce that code for you. Writing a good prompt—clear inputs, outputs, data contract, and constraints—is itself the skill.

Why vector search? Past incidents are matched by meaning, not keywords. The tool embeds the user's error into a vector and asks BigQuery for the nearest stored incidents by cosine distance. The one rule that must hold: the query and the stored rows must use the same embedding model (text-embedding-004, 768 dimensions), or the vectors live in different spaces and the distances are meaningless. The table was pre-seeded with text-embedding-004 vectors, so the prompt tells the generated agent to embed queries with the same model.

Why GKE? This specialist represents a team that standardized on Kubernetes. Deploying it to GKE proves the third runtime in the system; the root still reaches it as just another A2A card.

Install the lab tooling
Give your coding agent the agents-cli skills so it knows how to scaffold, build, and deploy an agents-cli project. This adds the skills to all detected AI coding assistants (Antigravity, Gemini, Claude Code, and others).

Set the project and region variables for this task (re-run these if you opened a new Cloud Shell tab).

export PROJECT_ID=PROJECT_ID
export REGION=us-central1
gcloud config set project ${PROJECT_ID}
Copied!
In the Cloud Shell Terminal, install the skills.

agents-cli setup --agent all
Copied!
Install Terraform, which the GKE deploy uses to provision the cluster.

wget -O - https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg
echo "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg] https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com $(grep -oP '(?<=UBUNTU_CODENAME=).*' /etc/os-release || lsb_release -cs) main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hashicorp.list
sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y terraform
terraform -version
Copied!
Generate the agent with Antigravity
Move to your agents workspace and launch the Antigravity CLI in the Cloud Shell Terminal.

cd ~/agents
agy
Copied!
When prompted to choose a login method, select Google Cloud Project, location global and follow the prompts to authenticate.

Paste the following prompt into Antigravity to generate the project under ~/agents/bq-agent, then review the files it creates.

 # Build a Bug Database agent (ADK + BigQuery semantic search, A2A, on GKE)

 Build a standalone agent named **`bq-agent`**. You have the **agents-cli** skills;
 use them to design, build, and deploy it. This is a description of the product and
 its capabilities, not a set of steps — decide the implementation yourself.

 ## What it is

 A self-contained specialist agent that answers one question well: *"Have we seen
 this software problem before, and how was it fixed?"* It performs **semantic
 (meaning-based) search** over an internal database of past engineering incidents
 in **BigQuery** and returns the most similar incidents with their resolutions.

 - Framework: **ADK** (Python), built with agents-cli.
 - Model: **`gemini-3.5-flash`** (read from config; don't hardcode).
 - Interface: **A2A** — a peer service with no knowledge of any specific caller.
 - Runtime: **GKE**.
 - Access: **read-only** over BigQuery. Never mutates incident data.

 ## The tool: `find_similar_bugs`

 One tool provides the entire competency.

 - **Signature:** `find_similar_bugs(query: str) -> str`
 - **Input:** a free-text problem description (exception, key stack-trace terms,
   language/framework, symptom).
 - **Retrieval:**
   1. Embed `query` with the same embedding model used for the stored vectors
     (`text-embedding-004`), in the table's region.
   2. Run BigQuery `VECTOR_SEARCH` over `description_embedding` with
     `distance_type => 'COSINE'`, `top_k => 3`, passing the query vector as a
     `FLOAT64` array parameter. Order results by ascending distance.
 - **Output:** for each match — `bug_id`, `title`, `description`, `resolution`,
   and the cosine distance. Return a clear "no similar bugs found" when the table
   yields nothing, and a graceful degradation message if embedding or BigQuery is
   temporarily unavailable (do not raise).

 ## The knowledge base (BigQuery)

 The table is **pre-populated** for you. Do **not** create, seed, or write to it —
 query it read-only against this schema.

 **Table `code_assist_bugs.known_bugs` (region `us-central1`):**

 | column | type | notes |
 |--------|------|-------|
 | `bug_id` | STRING | stable id; cite it in answers (e.g. `BUG-1001`) |
 | `title` | STRING | short incident title |
 | `description` | STRING | problem statement that was embedded |
 | `resolution` | STRING | the concrete fix |
 | `description_embedding` | `ARRAY<FLOAT64>` | embedding of `description`; length = the embedding model's dimension (768 for `text-embedding-004`) |

 - **Vector search:** cosine distance; a vector index is optional at this scale.
 - **Embedding compatibility:** the stored vectors were produced with
   **
Copied!
Note: Antigravity generates the project and can run agents-cli for you. Review the generated app/agent.py and app/tools.py before deploying. The BigQuery table code_assist_bugs.known_bugs was pre-populated when the lab started.
