Task 5. Orchestrate the specialists in a graph workflow
In this task, you build the brain of the system. The root Code Assist agent is an ADK 2.0 graph workflow that maps each specialist as an A2A node, fans out to them in parallel, joins their results, and synthesizes one cited answer. You test the whole system locally before deploying it.

Implement a callback for remote agents to save their findings to the session state
A RemoteA2aAgent has no output_key argument, so its final reply cannot automatically be saved to the session state. Here you'll complete a callback to copy its final text reply into state[state_key] so the synthesizer can read the remote agents' output from {state_key?}.

Open ~/agents/code-assistant/app/callbacks.py.

Complete the TODO to complete the capture_response_to_state function, which returns a _after_agent_callback callback function. This function should:

Get the events from the callback_context's session
For the most recent event where the event.author matches the callback_context.agent_name and the event content includes a text part, join all the text parts and save the results to the callback_context's state with the key 'state_key'.
Why the findings-to-state callback? A workflow node's output is captured only from an event's output field, which an LlmAgent sets but a RemoteA2aAgent does not. A remote agent emits content, not a captured output, so its branch would be empty at the join. Each remote node therefore uses an after_agent_callback to copy its final reply into shared state, and the synthesizer reads each specialist's findings from state by key. This is a subtle but important pattern when mixing remote agents into a graph.
Create an httpx client that authenticates using Google Application Default Credentials
In order for your ADK root_agent to call your github_agent on Agent Runtime via A2A, it needs to provide authentication headers. Here you will use the google_authed_client() helper function which uses Google Application Default Credentials to authenticate the request.

Open ~/agents/code-assistant/app/auth.py.

Within the GoogleADCAuth class, complete the TODO in the _apply method to attach the Google Application Default Credentials (ADC) OAuth2 token to the outgoing request headers.

To do this, the _apply() method will need to run the self._creds credential's before_request() method, passing in the required parameters. Running the before_request method refreshes the credentials if necessary, then applies the token to the authentication header.

You can see _apply() is applied to a request by the sync and async auth flows.

Construct the specialist agents
Open ~/agents/code-assistant/app/agent.py.

Notice that there is a section under a comment header # --- Remote specialists over A2A ----- which you will complete shortly to add additional specialist agents.

Below that, notice the section with the comment header # --- Graph nodes ----- which contains nodes already prepared for you, including:

a query_extractor to extract the primary issue from the user's input
a manual_search_agent which uses the Vertex AI Search datastore you created in Task 1
a merge node to join the results from all the specialists
a synthesizer to interpret the join results and produce a prioritized, cited answer
Further below, notice the root_agent of class Workflow() where you will complete the graph connections.

Returning to the Remote specialists section, complete the TODO to implement the github_agent as a RemoteA2aAgent. Its card will be found at the GITHUB_AGENT_URL.

For auth, github_agent should set the httpx_client parameter to use the google_authed_client() you implemented above.

For the github_agent, the RemoteA2aAgent constructor should have use_legacy set to False because it calls an ADK agent and can use the ADK A2A extension. (Note: Search "The ADK A2A extension" in this lab to revisit that discussion.)

Also for the github_agent, add an after_agent_callback that calls capture_response_to_state() with the state key 'github_findings'.

Now complete the TODO to implement the stackexchange_agent. It should be implemented as a CleanQueryRemoteA2aAgent with a name, description, agent_card of STACKEXCHANGE_AGENT_URL, and an after_agent_callback that calls capture_response_to_state with the state key 'stackexchange_findings'.

Note that the Stack Exchange node uses the legacy client (use_legacy is not set to False) because it calls a non-ADK LangGraph server.

Complete the root_agent Workflow graph
In the same file (code-assistant/app/agent.py), complete the TODO to complete the root_agent's Workflow graph. Implement the edges to connect START to query_extractor, fan out to all 5 specialists, merge them with the merge JoinNode, then connect merge to synthesizer.

Why a JoinNode? The specialists run concurrently and finish at different times. A JoinNode is a synchronization barrier: it waits for every incoming branch before the next node runs. A normal node used as a fan-in target would fire once per branch, so the synthesizer would run before the slower specialists returned. The JoinNode guarantees the synthesizer sees all results at once.
Configure the root's connections
Create the root agent's .env, pulling in the specialist card URLs you exported in the previous tasks. Leave SALESFORCE_AGENT_URL as a placeholder for now; you add Salesforce in Task 7.

cd ~/agents/code-assistant
cat << EOF > .env
GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=PROJECT_ID
GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION=global
GOOGLE_GENAI_USE_VERTEXAI=True
MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash
DATASTORE_ID=code-manuals-datastore
DEVELOPER_KNOWLEDGE_MCP_URL=https://developerknowledge.googleapis.com/mcp
SKILL_REGISTRY_LOCATION=us-central1
GITHUB_AGENT_URL=${GITHUB_AGENT_URL}
STACKEXCHANGE_AGENT_URL=${STACKEXCHANGE_AGENT_URL}
SALESFORCE_AGENT_URL=added-in-task-7
BQ_AGENT_URL=${BQ_AGENT_URL}
EOF
Copied!
Note: manual_search now has two sources: the internal handbook datastore (DATASTORE_ID) and Google's official developer docs via the Developer Knowledge MCP (DEVELOPER_KNOWLEDGE_MCP_URL), whose guiding skill lives in the Skill Registry (SKILL_REGISTRY_LOCATION, a regional service, so us-central1 even though the rest of the lab uses global).
Confirm the specialist URLs were filled in and none are blank. This check flags any empty URL now, so the root deploy in Task 6 doesn't fail with a cryptic "Required field is not set" error.

cd ~/agents/code-assistant
blank=$(grep -E '^(GITHUB_AGENT_URL|STACKEXCHANGE_AGENT_URL|BQ_AGENT_URL)=$' .env)
if [ -n "${blank}" ]; then echo "BLANK specialist URLs (re-export them and recreate .env):"; echo "${blank}"; else echo "All specialist URLs are set."; fi
Copied!
Note: The URLs come from the GITHUB_AGENT_URL, STACKEXCHANGE_AGENT_URL, and BQ_AGENT_URL variables you exported earlier. If the check lists any as blank (for example, after reopening Cloud Shell), re-export it from the value you recorded in that specialist's task, then recreate the file.
