Task 6. Deploy the root and register it in Gemini Enterprise
In this task, you deploy the root agent to Agent Runtime, authorize it to call the GitHub specialist, verify the full system, and publish it to Gemini Enterprise so users can reach it.

Deploy the root agent
Set the project and region variables for this task (re-run these if you opened a new Cloud Shell tab).

export PROJECT_ID=PROJECT_ID
export REGION=us-central1
gcloud config set project ${PROJECT_ID}
Copied!
Deploy the root to Agent Runtime. Remove the local .venv first, then deploy.

cd ~/agents/code-assistant
rm -rf .venv
agents-cli deploy --project ${PROJECT_ID} --no-confirm-project --no-wait \
  --update-env-vars "$(grep -v '^#' .env | grep -v '^$' | grep -v '^GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT' | paste -sd,)"
Copied!
Deploy failing with an auth error? If you see service account info is missing 'email' field, your Application Default Credentials lapsed. Run gcloud auth application-default login, then re-run the deploy. If you see a gcloud reauthentication or "no active account" error instead, run gcloud auth login and re-run the deploy. If the failure persists, click Restart in the Cloud Shell toolbar and re-run this task's export block first.
Why remove .venv? Testing locally in Task 5 created a .venv virtual environment in the project. Agent Runtime deploys by packaging your project directory into an archive, and .venv/bin/python is a symlink that points to a Python outside the project, which the packager rejects. You lose nothing by deleting it, because Agent Runtime rebuilds dependencies from pyproject.toml and uv.lock on the server. Normally you wouldn't remove it by hand: a .dockerignore (or .gitignore, which agents-cli honors when packaging) excludes .venv automatically. This starter project doesn't ship one, so you clear it manually here.
Poll until the deployment reports success.

agents-cli deploy --status
Copied!
Authorize the root agent
Here is where the local-versus-deployed identity difference becomes real. When you tested in the playground (Task 5), the root called GitHub and the datastore with your user credentials, which already have access, so it worked. Now the root runs on Agent Runtime and calls them as its runtime service account instead. That service account presents a valid token, but it has not been granted permission to invoke the GitHub engine or query the datastore, so those calls return 403 Permission Denied (a valid token, insufficient permission), not 401.

The fix is two IAM grants: roles/aiplatform.user (it includes aiplatform.reasoningEngines.query, needed to call the GitHub agent, and also lets the manual_search branch load its skill from the Skill Registry) and roles/discoveryengine.viewer (needed for the manual_search branch to query the code-manuals datastore). This is the authorization layer described in the concepts section: authentication proves who you are, authorization decides what you may call.

Provision the runtime service account so you can grant it roles. Agent Runtime uses the AI Platform Reasoning Engine Service Agent, which may not exist yet in a new project. Create it explicitly.

gcloud beta services identity create --service=aiplatform.googleapis.com --project=${PROJECT_ID}
Copied!
Note: The command output may name only the Vertex AI Service Agent, but it also creates the Reasoning Engine Service Agent (service-PROJECT_NUMBER@gcp-sa-aiplatform-re.iam.gserviceaccount.com), which is the identity you grant roles to next.
Grant the runtime service account both roles.

export PROJECT_NUMBER=$(gcloud projects describe ${PROJECT_ID} --format='value(projectNumber)')
gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding ${PROJECT_ID} \
  --member="serviceAccount:service-${PROJECT_NUMBER}@gcp-sa-aiplatform-re.iam.gserviceaccount.com" \
  --role="roles/aiplatform.user"
gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding ${PROJECT_ID} \
  --member="serviceAccount:service-${PROJECT_NUMBER}@gcp-sa-aiplatform-re.iam.gserviceaccount.com" \
  --role="roles/discoveryengine.viewer"
Copied!
If a grant fails with Service account service-...@gcp-sa-aiplatform-re.iam.gserviceaccount.com does not exist: the service agent hasn't finished provisioning. Re-run the gcloud beta services identity create command from the previous step, wait a few seconds, then re-run the grants.
Note: IAM changes take a minute or two to take effect. If the first end-to-end call fails to resolve the GitHub agent or query the datastore, wait and try again.
Developer Knowledge (official Google docs): the manual_search branch also calls the Developer Knowledge MCP as the runtime service account. Its API is enabled for the project (developerknowledge.googleapis.com), and roles/aiplatform.user covers loading the skill from the Skill Registry. If the deployed root returns internal-handbook results but no official-docs citations, confirm that API is enabled and check the deployment logs for the MCP call.
