Create the Gemini Enterprise app
Deploying makes the root reachable over A2A, but end users don't call A2A endpoints directly. Gemini Enterprise is the web app where users discover and chat with your agent, and where the platform applies enterprise access controls. An agent on Agent Runtime does not appear there automatically, so you first create an app, then register the agent into it.

You create the app in the console, not through the API. Creating it in the UI completes the Gemini Enterprise setup the backend needs before it allocates quota for agent creation. Creating the app through the API skips that step, so a later publish fails.

In the Google Cloud console, search for Gemini Enterprise in the top search bar and open it.
If prompted, accept the terms and click Continue to enable Gemini Enterprise for the project.
Click Create your first app (or Create app).
For the app name, enter enterprise-app.
Under the name, click Edit next to the generated App ID and replace it with enterprise-app. The publish command in the next section targets .../engines/enterprise-app, so the ID must match exactly.
Choose global for the location, then click Create.
Configure the identity provider for Gemini Enterprise
Go to the Gemini Enterprise page.

Click on Settings > Authentication.

Click Edit for the global location.

In the Add identity provider dialog, click Google Identity.

Click Save.

Note: If the console kept a suffix on the App ID (for example, enterprise-app_1699...), that's fine. Copy your actual App ID and use it in place of enterprise-app in the publish command in the next section.
Register and share the agent in Gemini Enterprise
Registering publishes the agent to a governed catalog where users can discover and chat with it. It is the last step that turns your deployed system into a product people can use.

Register the deployed root into your app. Run this from the agent directory so the CLI auto-detects the reasoning engine from deployment_metadata.json, and pass only the app ID.

cd ~/agents/code-assistant
agents-cli publish gemini-enterprise \
  --gemini-enterprise-app-id "projects/${PROJECT_NUMBER}/locations/global/collections/default_collection/engines/enterprise-app" \
  --display-name "Code Assist" \
  --description "Researches a production error across a bug database, the engineering handbook, Salesforce, GitHub, and Stack Exchange, and returns one cited fix."
Copied!
In the Gemini Enterprise console, open the enterprise-app app, select Agents, and confirm Code Assist is listed.

Select Code Assist, then open the User permissions tab.

Select Add user, add your lab user (or All users), choose the Agent User role, and select Save.

In the left navigation, select the Integration tab, then open the link under The link to your web app.

In the web app, choose Code Assist in the agents window, then ask a question that spans several sources, such as the following.

A CloudSQL connection fails with "too many connections" under load. Have we hit this incident before, what does the engineering handbook recommend, and what are the known fixes?
Copied!
Confirm the reply is a single, cited answer that blends multiple sources: past incidents from the bug database, the internal engineering handbook, and the web specialists (GitHub and Stack Exchange).

