Deploy the Salesforce agent to Cloud Run

Task 8. Give the Salesforce agent long-term memory with Vertex AI Memory Bank
So far, every agent forgets a user the moment a conversation ends. Each A2A call starts a fresh session, so the Salesforce agent cannot recall what a user told it last time. In this task, you add long-term memory so the agent remembers durable facts about each user across separate conversations.

Agents use two kinds of memory. Short-term memory is the session: the messages in the current conversation. Long-term memory outlives the session. Vertex AI Memory Bank is a managed long-term memory service on Agent Runtime. After each turn it reads the session, uses a model to extract durable facts about the user, and stores them. On the next turn it returns those facts so the agent can personalize its answer.

The Salesforce agent already ships with the code to use Memory Bank. In this task you provision a Memory Bank, connect it, review how the agent reads, writes, and scopes memory, and then test recall across two conversations.

Complete TODOs to add memory to the Salesforce agent
Open the file code-assistant/app/app_utils/a2a.py.
Complete the TODO to return the real end-user id to be included in A2A metadata: {'user_id': ctx.user_id}. This allows you to load longterm memories for a user, rather than relying only on context within a single session.
Open the file salesforce-agent/scripts/create_memory_bank.py.
Complete the TODO to complete the main() function. It doesn't need to return anything, but should be idempotent (i.e., if you run it multiple times, it should not create multiple memory banks). It should first look for an existing memory bank with a display name that matches DISPLAY_NAME (by listing existing agent_engines, as a Memory Bank is created by default with an agent_engine). If it does not find one, create one. Either way, print the display name and whether it was found or is being created.
Review how the agent reads, writes, and scopes memory
Open ~/agents/salesforce-agent/app/agent.py and find PreloadMemoryTool, which loads the user's remembered facts before each turn, and generate_memories_callback, which calls add_session_to_memory to save the conversation after each turn.

Open ~/agents/salesforce-agent/app/app_utils/services.py and note that the memory service is a VertexAiMemoryBankService when MEMORY_BANK_ID is set, and an in-memory service otherwise.

Open ~/agents/code-assistant/app/app_utils/a2a.py and find user_id_meta_provider, which forwards the caller's user ID to a specialist over A2A.

Open ~/agents/salesforce-agent/app/app_utils/a2a.py and find _user_scoped_request_converter, which reads that forwarded user ID so memory is scoped to the real user.

Why the user ID matters: memory is stored per user (per app_name and user_id). Over A2A, an unauthenticated call carries no user identity, so the specialist would fall back to a per-conversation ID (A2A_USER_{context_id}) and forget the user in the next conversation. The root forwards its real user ID so recall works across conversations.
Provision a Memory Bank and connect the agent
Set the project and region variables for this task (re-run these if you opened a new Cloud Shell tab).

export PROJECT_ID=PROJECT_ID
export REGION=us-central1
gcloud config set project ${PROJECT_ID}
Copied!
Create a Memory Bank instance for the Salesforce agent.

cd ~/agents/salesforce-agent
GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=${PROJECT_ID} 
MEMORY_BANK_LOCATION=us-central1
uv run python scripts/create_memory_bank.py
Copied!
Copy the MEMORY_BANK_ID value from the script output.

Open ~/agents/salesforce-agent/.env and add two lines to turn on persistent memory: MEMORY_BANK_ID=<the value you copied> and MEMORY_BANK_LOCATION=us-central1.

Redeploy the Salesforce agent so it connects to the Memory Bank.

agents-cli deploy --project ${PROJECT_ID} --region ${REGION} --no-confirm-project \
  --update-env-vars "$(grep -v '^#' .env | grep -v '^$' | grep -v '^GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT' | paste -sd,)"
Copied!
Deploy failing with an auth error? If you see service account info is missing 'email' field, your Application Default Credentials lapsed. Run gcloud auth application-default login, then re-run the deploy. If you see a gcloud reauthentication or "no active account" error instead, run gcloud auth login and re-run the deploy. If the failure persists, click Restart in the Cloud Shell toolbar and re-run this task's export block first.
Grant public access again, because redeploying resets the service's IAM policy.

gcloud run services add-iam-policy-binding salesforce-agent \
  --region=${REGION} --member=allUsers --role=roles/run.invoker
Copied!
Redeploy the root agent (code-assistant) to Agent Runtime.

cd ~/agents/code-assistant
rm -rf .venv
agents-cli deploy --project ${PROJECT_ID} --no-confirm-project --no-wait \
  --update-env-vars "$(grep -v '^#' .env | grep -v '^$' | grep -v '^GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT' | paste -sd,)"
Copied!
Note: Memory Bank uses a regional endpoint. Keep MEMORY_BANK_LOCATION=us-central1 even though the agent's model runs on the global endpoint, because the global endpoint does not serve Memory Bank.
Test long-term, per-user memory
In Gemini Enterprise, start a conversation and state a durable fact about yourself, such as I'm on the Payments team and I only care about Spanner documents.

Wait about a minute for Memory Bank to generate the memory, then start a new conversation.

In the new conversation, ask a related question without repeating the fact, and confirm the agent recalls your preference.

Memory stores facts about you, not your questions. Memory Bank keeps durable facts about the user, so a troubleshooting question like "why does my service return 502?" records nothing on its own. A memory appears only when you say something lasting about yourself, such as your team, your role, or the sources you care about. This is by design: it keeps memory relevant instead of cluttered with one-off queries.
Generation is asynchronous and selective. Writes happen in the background after the turn, so a new memory takes a short time to appear. A model also consolidates and de-duplicates facts, so repeated statements do not create duplicates.
View your stored memories in the Cloud Console
In the Google Cloud console, go to Agent Engines (Agent Platform > Agents > Memory Bank).

Open the salesforce-agent-memory engine.

Open the Memories tab and confirm your fact appears, scoped to your user ID (your email).

Tip: You can jump straight to the memories view at https://console.cloud.google.com/agent-platform/runtimes/locations/us-central1/agent-engines/[MEMORY_BANK_ID]/memories?project=${PROJECT_ID}, using the Memory Bank ID from the create step.
